// For editors, not for CI.
//
// An editor resolves a file against the NEAREST tsconfig.json. The root one
// includes `src/**/*.ts` and excludes `**/*.test.ts`, because the build emits
// only src to dist — so nothing under tests/ belonged to any project, and the
// editor fell back to an inferred one with default options. That project never
// loads `tests/support/n3.d.ts`, so every file importing `n3` showed "Could not
// find a declaration file for module 'n3'" while `npm run typecheck` was clean.
//
// This exists so that stops. Extending the typecheck config gives the editor
// exactly the project CI checks; `include` paths from an extended config are
// resolved against the config that declared them, not this one, so the
// allow-list there still means what it says.
//
// `rootDir` is the one addition, and it is not cosmetic. Unset, it is inferred
// from where the config sits, so the language service reported every `src/` file
// as "not under rootDir 'tests'" — trading one spurious error for another. The
// program genuinely spans src/ and tests/, and the package root is where they
// meet. `tsc -p` never complained either way, because it is the editor's
// language service that validates this.
{
  "extends": "../tsconfig.typecheck.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "rootDir": ".."
  }
}
